== 'prerequisite calculus' attributions (pages and cards)
course/modules/M/M2/L22.html:94:        <p><strong>Assumption 1.1 (derivative of the logarithm).</strong> For \(a > 0\), \(\tfrac{d}{da}\ln a = 1/a\). This page takes the fact from the prerequisite calculus and does not prove it.</p>
course/modules/M/M2/L24.html:132:      <p><strong>Assumption 4.1 (derivative of the exponential).</strong> For every real \(x\), \(\tfrac{d}{dx}e^x = e^x\). This page takes the fact from the prerequisite calculus and does not prove it. The lab's predicted derivative at \(x_0 = 1\), the value \(e\), is stated under this assumption.</p>
course/modules/M/M2/L24.html:136:        <p class="hypothesis">SIMULATED: both functions are test functions chosen for this lab, not market data. Part A is \(|x|\) at \(0\), predicted before running by Theorem 2.1: the forward quotient is \(+1\), the backward quotient is \(-1\), and the central quotient is \(0\), at every step \(10^{-k}\). Part B is \(e^x\) at \(x_0 = 1\), whose derivative is \(e\). Assuming Assumption 4.1, the derivative of \(e^x\) at \(1\) is \(e\); the assumption is \(\tfrac{d}{dx}e^x = e^x\), taken from the prerequisite calculus and not proved on this page. Predicted before running: the forward error falls while the truncation term \(\tfrac12 e\, h\) dominates, then rises as the round-off term grows. The model of Proposition 4.2 with \(M_2 \approx e\) and an assumed \(\delta = 2.22 \times 10^{-16}\), a half unit in the last place of values near \(e\), gives a minimizer near \(2\sqrt{\delta/e} \approx 1.8 \times 10^{-8}\) for the forward quotient. Proposition 4.3 with \(M_3 \approx e\) gives a minimizer near \((3\delta/e)^{1/3} \approx 6.3 \times 10^{-6}\) for the central quotient. The lab prints the grid decade with the smallest error for each. The values of \(\delta\), \(M_2\) and \(M_3\) are assumed here, not measured.</p>
course/modules/M/M2/cards.json:2716:  "back": "For a > 0, the derivative of ln a is 1/a. It is taken from prerequisite calculus and not proved; Example 1.5 uses it."
course/modules/M/M2/cards.json:2722:  "back": "For every real x, the derivative of e^x is e^x. It is taken from prerequisite calculus and not proved; the value e at x0 = 1 relies on it."

